#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:20
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Infrared Film</emphasis> filter simulates "
"traditional infrared film material."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: editor-filters.docbook:26 editor-filters.docbook:98
#: editor-filters.docbook:167 editor-filters.docbook:228
#: editor-filters.docbook:289 editor-filters.docbook:353
#: editor-filters.docbook:596 editor-filters.docbook:775
#, no-c-format
msgid "<keyword>KDE</keyword>"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: keyword
#: editor-filters.docbook:27 editor-filters.docbook:99
#: editor-filters.docbook:168 editor-filters.docbook:229
#: editor-filters.docbook:290 editor-filters.docbook:354
#: editor-filters.docbook:597 editor-filters.docbook:776
#, no-c-format
msgid "Digikam"
msgstr "Digikam"

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:32 editor-filters.docbook:104
#: editor-filters.docbook:173 editor-filters.docbook:234
#: editor-filters.docbook:293 editor-filters.docbook:359
#: editor-filters.docbook:602 editor-filters.docbook:781
#, no-c-format
msgid "Introduction"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:34
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Simulating classical infrared film material (the effect is rendered in black "
"and white) is an interesting alienation effect that is even stronger and "
"more dramatic than pure black and white conversion. Contrast and an abstract "
"touch are improved, which can underpin the expression of the photographer as "
"an artist. It is like taking an image in black and white with a red filter "
"on the camera lenses. Areas which reflect little red light, &eg; the sky, "
"will show as low density, dark areas. Areas which are excellent reflectors "
"of red light, e.g. most green foliage, will be high density areas. And snow "
"landscapes are really dramatic."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:40
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The algorithm is based on the method of the 'Simulate Infrared Film' "
"tutorial of the GimpGuru.org web site available at <ulink url=\"http://www."
"gimpguru.org/Tutorials/SimulatedInfrared\">this url</ulink>. The filter "
"tries to reproduce the famous Ilford(tm) SFX200 infrared film. This film has "
"a sensitivity range of 200-800 ISO."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:46
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using the Infrared Filter"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:48
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The left part of the dialog window lets you select the region to be shown in "
"the preview section. Move the red rectangle with the mouse around to show "
"the effect on different parts of the image."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:52
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The lower part provides the two controls, Film Grain and ISO sensitivity. "
"Check the <guilabel>Add Film Grain</guilabel> box if you want to simulate "
"the grainy texture of a high sensitivity film. The ISO-level slider modifies "
"the amount of filmgrain added and the predominance of green color "
"(chlorophyll in nature) in the conversion mixer. Green pastures will become "
"white as snow! Try it out."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:56
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Because the filter mixes color channels to reproduce infrared film (with "
"emphasis on green channel), one can not simulate infrared effect from black "
"and white original photograph, since the color information is missing."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:63 editor-filters.docbook:130
#, no-c-format
msgid "The filter in action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:65
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is an example of the infrared film effect applied to a color image "
"taken in New Zealand's landscapes. The original image is (1) and the "
"converted image is (2). The film sensitivity used to simulate the infrared "
"film is ISO-400. Higher ISO values will create a kind of aura in the "
"highlights."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: editor-filters.docbook:71
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Infrared filter in Action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:81
#, no-c-format
msgid "Film Grain"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:91
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Filmgrain</emphasis> filter reproduces traditional "
"film grain techniques of high speed films."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:106
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Filmgrain</emphasis> filter is an easy tool to "
"produce film grain on your images as known from classical high speed film "
"material as, for example, the famous B/W KodaK Tri-X. In order to increase "
"film sensitivity, manufacturers employed larger silver grains in the photo "
"emulsion."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:112
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The film grain effect gives your shot a particular mood or seems to "
"transport it in time. The treated image acquires a timeless atmosphere, "
"detached from every day life. If you want that gritty, art-house, street-"
"photography grainy film look, especially in monochromatic photos, use this "
"filter."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:119
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using the Filmgrain filter"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:121
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There is a slider calibrated in ISO-sensitivity to control the grain "
"intensity and granularity. The default is set to ISO-2400, although higher "
"values are often required. If you still want more grain, apply the filter "
"several times."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:132
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is an example of the film grain effect applied on a black and white "
"image. The original image is (1) and the corrected image is (2). The film "
"sensitivity used for simulate the film graininess is ISO-1600."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: editor-filters.docbook:140
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Film Grain Filter in Action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:150
#, no-c-format
msgid "Oil Paint"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:160
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Oil Paint</emphasis> filter gives your image the "
"look of an oilpainting. It uses an algorithm copyrighted by Pieter Voloshyn."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:175
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Oil Paint</emphasis> filter gives your digital "
"images a nice oilpainting-like look. Images of nature and still lifes are "
"well suited for this effect."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:181
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using the Oil Paint filter"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:183
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are two sliders to control the effect. The upper slider selects the "
"<guilabel>Brush Size</guilabel> between 1 and 5. Bigger brushes are better "
"suited for large images. <guilabel>Smooth</guilabel> controls the smoothness "
"or, seen from the other end, the jaggedness."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:192
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Oil Paint filter in action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:194
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is an example of the oil paint filter effect. The original image is (1) "
"and the transformed image is (2). <guilabel>Brush Size</guilabel> for this "
"640 pixel size image is 1, <guilabel>Smooth</guilabel> is 17."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: editor-filters.docbook:202
#, no-c-format
msgid "Oil Paint Image Effect Preview"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:212
#, no-c-format
msgid "Charcoal Drawing"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:222
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Charcoal</emphasis> is an effect filter that creates "
"a charcoal sketch-like result."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:236
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Charcoal</emphasis> filter uses the gradients of "
"color and luminosity to produce a grey scale charcoal sketch. The lines "
"defining the outline of the image are pronounced. Images with slowly "
"changing gradients are not ideal for this effect. It is helpful to imagine "
"what scene you would pick to do as a hand sketch yourself, in order to "
"choose the image to start with."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:244
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using the Charcoal filter"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:246
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"There are two sliders to control the effect on a scale of 1-100. The upper "
"slider selects the pencil size, whereas the second slider adjusts the "
"contrast (smoothness)."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:254
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Charcoal filter in action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:256
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is an example of the charcoal filter. The original image is (1) and the "
"transformed image is (2). Default values of 30 and 10 where applied. The "
"result can be improved by adjusting the luminosity levels."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: editor-filters.docbook:263
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Charcoal filter in Action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:273
#, no-c-format
msgid "Emboss Image"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:283
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Emboss</emphasis> filter sculptures your image into "
"3-D as if it were stamped into wax. It uses an algorithm copyrighted by "
"Pieter Voloshyn."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:295
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Emboss</emphasis> filter is a quick tool to render "
"your images in a 3-D effect. It works particularly well on images with "
"simple structure where color is not the most important content. The filter "
"uses the difference between colors and luminosity to convert it into a grey, "
"moon-like landscape lit from 10 o'clock."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:302
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using the Emboss filter"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:304
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The <guilabel>Depth</guilabel> control allows to define the contrast of the "
"filtering. A value of 30 (10%) is the standard."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:311
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Emboss filter in action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:313
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"This is an example of the emboss filter. The original image is (1) and the "
"transformed image is (2). The <guilabel>Depth</guilabel> applied is 10%."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: phrase
#: editor-filters.docbook:320
#, no-c-format
msgid "The Emboss filter in Action"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:330
#, no-c-format
msgid "Distortion FX"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:347
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"The &digikam; <emphasis>Distortion FX</emphasis> is a series of distorting "
"effects for digital still images."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:361
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"With this filter set, you can transform an ordinary photograph into a work "
"of art suitable for framing using distorting operations. It uses algorithms "
"copyrighted by Pieter Voloshyn."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: title
#: editor-filters.docbook:366
#, no-c-format
msgid "Using the filter"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:368
#, no-c-format
msgid "These are the distorting effects available:"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:378 editor-filters.docbook:621
#, no-c-format
msgid "Type"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:379 editor-filters.docbook:622
#, no-c-format
msgid "Preview"
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:386
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Fish Eyes</guilabel>: warps the photograph around a 3D spherical "
"shape to reproduce the common photograph 'Fish Eyes' effect."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:396
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Twirl</guilabel>: spins the photograph to produce a Twirl pattern."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:406
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Cylinder Horizontal</guilabel>: warps the photograph around a "
"horizontal cylinder."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:416
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Cylinder Vertical</guilabel>: warps the photograph around a "
"vertical cylinder."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:426
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Cylinder H/V</guilabel>: warps the photograph around a 2 "
"cylinders, vertical and horizontal."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:436
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Caricature</guilabel>: distorts photograph with 'Fish Eyes' effect "
"inverted."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:446
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Multiple Corners</guilabel>: splits the photograph like a multiple "
"corners pattern."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:456
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Waves Horizontal</guilabel>: distorts the photograph with "
"horizontal waves."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:466
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Waves Vertical</guilabel>: distorts the photograph with vertical "
"waves."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:476
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Block Waves 1</guilabel>: divides the image into cells and makes "
"it look as if it is being viewed through glass blocks."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:486
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Block Waves 2</guilabel>: like Block Waves 1 but with another "
"version of glass blocks distortion."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:496
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Circular Waves 1</guilabel>: distorts the photograph with circular "
"waves."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:506
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Circular Waves 2</guilabel>: other variation of Circular Waves "
"effect."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:516
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Polar Coordinates</guilabel>: converts the photograph from "
"rectangular to polar coordinates."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:525
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Unpolar Coordinates</guilabel>: Polar Coordinate effect inverted."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: entry
#: editor-filters.docbook:535
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"<guilabel>Tiles</guilabel>: splits the photograph into square blocks and "
"move them randomly inside the image."
msgstr ""

#. Tag: para
#: editor-filters.docbook:550
#, no-c-format
msgid ""
"Some effects can take a long time to run and cause high CPU load. You can "
"always abort an effect by pressing <guilabel>Abort</guilabel> button during "
"preview rendering."
msgstr ""

